  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1132 PM CST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nSaturday night through Thursday:\n\nThe main focus on the long term is with the precipitation that\nmoves in very early on Sunday morning and exits southeastern WI\nin the early afternoon hours...followed by a general warming\ntrend through the rest of next week.\n\nA developing mid level trough currently noted on WV in the\nPacific NW will dig southeast riding the mean flow into the\nmid-lower Mississippi River Valley region into Sunday morning. \nAs this trough digs and closes off Sunday morning, a surface low\nwill track from Oklahoma through southern Illinois and Ohio on \nSunday. Deep lift and expanding low level moisture transport out\nof the Gulf will help to spread the precipitation shield \nnorthward of the low and ahead of the trough into southern \nWisconsin early on Sunday. As the 28.00z guidance has started to\ncome in, it continues to show that there may be a southward \njaunt to the precip shield as the low closes off and stays a bit\nfurther south. Regardless, still a solid shot that we're going \nto see some precipitation out of this system with thermal \nprofiles showing that this will likely be more rain than a mix \nor snow. That said, it'll be close with surface temps in the mid\nto upper 30s and lower 40s as there will not be a warm layer \naloft due to the 850mb low staying south of the region. Best \nchances for any changeover appears to be on the northwestern \nfringe of the precipitation where temps will likely be a few \ndegrees cooler. Given the above freezing surface temps and road \ntemps still being above freezing, expect that if any snow does \nfall that there won't be impacts/accums from it based on the \ninformation in front of us at the moment.\n\nBroad ridging then pushes in behind this system for Monday with\nreturn southerly flow setting up for much of the week behind\nthis ridge passage. Low confidence in where the next system will\ntrack late in the week after it emerges from the SW US.\n\nHalbach",
